# The M2 gate, as a re-runnable script: three complete rounds against three AI
# opponents, with the frame rate measured rather than asserted.
#
#   python3 -m http.server 8000 --directory web/dist-m1 &
#   node web/tools/drive-browser.mjs --headed --out /tmp/gate-chrome \
#        --script-file web/tools/gameplay-gate.steps
#   node web/tools/drive-firefox.mjs          --out /tmp/gate-firefox \
#        --script-file web/tools/gameplay-gate.steps
#   kill %1
#
# --headed is REQUIRED for Chrome and only for Chrome: headless Chrome 152
# floods the page with thousands of spurious keydown events per real keypress
# and drowns the SDL event queue. Both drivers' header comments explain it.
#
# WHAT THIS DRIVES. A first-time visitor's path, unmodified -- no FIRST_USE 0.
# Play, language menu, First Setup, welcome message, and then the single-player
# match welcome() (gArmagetron.cpp:378-395) starts. With the three SP_* settings
# in web/webdefaults/autoexec.cfg that match is three rounds against three AIs.
#
# The match's parameters are NOT the shipped single-player ones, and nothing
# reporting on this run should pretend otherwise: welcome()
# (gArmagetron.cpp:378-395) temporarily sets speedFactor -2, sizeFactor -5
# (its own -3 default minus 2), wallsLength 400, rubber 5, delayCycle 0.05 for
# this one match and restores them afterwards. It is a real game of Armagetron
# with real AI opponents and real rounds; it is a gentle one, and a frame rate
# measured here is a TUTORIAL-PARAMETER frame rate.
#
# What welcome() does NOT touch is numAIs and limitRounds. So the three
# opponents are attributable to SP_NUM_AIS 3 alone and the three rounds to
# SP_LIMIT_ROUNDS 3 alone; only SP_AUTO_AIS is masked here, because welcome()
# forces autoNum to 0 for this match regardless.
#
# WHAT PASSES
#
#   Rounds: three "[L] ROUND_WINNER" lines -- NOT three "[L] NEW_ROUND" lines,
#   which count rounds STARTED.
#
#   Opponents: THE AI TEAM'S ROSTER, REPLAYED. Do not count
#   "[L] TEAM_PLAYER_ADDED ai_team ..." lines per round -- an earlier version of
#   this comment said to, and it is wrong. The TEAM_* writers log the DELTA, not
#   a census: round 1 logs three adds, round 2 logs one remove and one add, and
#   round 3 logs NOTHING AT ALL because its roster was already correct. Counting
#   adds reports round 3 as having zero opponents. Replay
#   TEAM_CREATED/TEAM_DESTROYED/TEAM_PLAYER_ADDED/TEAM_PLAYER_REMOVED into a set
#   instead; docs/evidence/m2-gate/check-transcript.mjs does exactly that and is
#   the reference implementation. "[L] ROUND_WINNER <team> <p1> <p2> <p3>" is an
#   independent second census, because the engine appends the winning team's
#   full membership (gGame.cpp:3944-3946 -> eTeam::WritePlayers).
#
#   Frame rate: a dump whose MINIMUM, not only whose median, clears 30.
#
#   Cleanliness: no "Stack overflow detected", no [EXCEPTION], no
#   "SDL event queue full", no "[GLERR]", no "until TIMED OUT", and every 404
#   being /favicon.ico.
#
# check-transcript.mjs checks every one of those against a transcript and exits
# non-zero if any fails. Prefer running it to reading a report that quotes it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Frame-rate sampler. Installed before anything runs, so nothing is missed.
#
# WHAT IS COUNTED AND WHY IT IS THE FRAME RATE. rSysDep::SwapGL()
# (src/render/rSysdep.cpp:747-758) ends every rendered frame with exactly one
# of glFlush() or glFinish(), chosen by swapMode_, immediately before the
# buffer swap. Emscripten compiles both to a single method call on the live
# WebGL context (`_glFlush = () => GLctx.flush()`, armagetronad.js:14460-14464),
# and they are the ONLY two calls to those methods in the whole program
# (`grep -rn 'glFlush\|glFinish' src/` finds rSysdep.cpp and a menu label).
# So one call through this wrapper == one frame the game finished drawing.
# Both are wrapped because swapMode_ is glFlush by default (rSysdep.cpp:459)
# and sr_LoadDefaultConfig() switches it to glFinish (rScreen.cpp:1029), which
# is what the first-use path this script drives actually runs with.
#
# This is a strictly better measurement than M1's setTimeout counter, which
# assumed two timeouts per frame. That assumption is only true while the frame
# finishes EARLY: sr_LimitFPS() (rSysdep.cpp:598-624) issues its
# emscripten_sleep only when there is time left in the frame budget, so the
# estimator silently halves exactly when the frame rate drops -- the one case
# a gate cares about.
#
# The same expression also timestamps every "[L] " ladder-log line in
# performance.now() time, because the round boundaries have to be expressed in
# the same clock as the frame samples for the window to mean anything. Module
# print routes stdout through console.log (web/shell.html:160) and resolves
# `console` at call time, so replacing it here catches everything the game
# prints; the original is kept bound, so CDP/BiDi still see every line.
#
# AND IT POLLS glGetError, WHICH IS THE PART THAT MAKES FIREFOX'S TRANSCRIPT
# MEAN SOMETHING. Chrome reports WebGL misuse as a browser-level Log entry that
# CDP forwards; Firefox 154 emits nothing over BiDi for the same call, which is
# measured at the bottom of this file and not assumed. A GL error read back
# from the context itself is engine-independent: it is the same WebGL state
# machine in both browsers, so the same defect produces the same line.
#
# Safe because the game never reads it. sr_CheckGLError() (rGL.cpp:36) is the
# only glGetError caller in the tree and it compiles to an empty inline unless
# AA_GL_ERROR_CHECKING is defined, which needs DEBUG, which this build does not
# define -- confirmed against the artifact rather than the headers: the string
# "glGetError" does not appear in armagetronad.wasm at all. So nothing else can
# have its error stolen by this poll.
#
# Every 30th frame, i.e. about twice a second, NOT every frame: getError() is a
# synchronous round trip in both engines and polling it 60 times a second would
# depress the very number this script exists to measure. The cost of that
# choice is that a single isolated GL error can be missed; the defect class
# this is watching for (browser-runtime-notes.md section 9 and 10) produces one
# per frame for as long as the offending draw path is live, so it cannot hide
# from a 2 Hz poll for more than half a second.
wait:1000
eval:(()=>{if(window.__fps)return 'already installed';const S={t:new Float64Array(400000),n:0,marks:[],gl:[],polls:0};window.__fps=S;const wrap=(C)=>{if(!C)return;for(const m of ['flush','finish']){const o=C.prototype[m];if(!o)continue;C.prototype[m]=function(){if(S.n<S.t.length)S.t[S.n++]=performance.now();if(S.n%30===0){S.polls++;const e=this.getError();if(e){S.gl.push([S.n,e]);if(S.gl.length<=20)console.error('[GLERR] frame '+S.n+' glGetError=0x'+e.toString(16));}}return o.apply(this,arguments);};}};wrap(window.WebGLRenderingContext);wrap(window.WebGL2RenderingContext);const cl=console.log.bind(console);console.log=function(){const s=(arguments.length&&typeof arguments[0]==='string')?arguments[0]:'';if(s.indexOf('[L] ')===0)S.marks.push([performance.now(),s]);return cl.apply(null,arguments);};return 'frame sampler armed: glFlush+glFinish counted, glGetError polled every 30th frame, [L] lines timestamped'})()
wait:7000
eval:(()=>{const c=document.getElementById('canvas');return 'canvas '+c.width+'x'+c.height})()
click:#start
wait:6000
mark:first-run-flow-language-menu
shot:01-language-menu
key:Enter
wait:3000
shot:02-first-setup-menu
mark:escape-accepts-the-defaults-and-leaves-first-setup
key:Escape
wait:4000
shot:03-welcome-message
mark:enter-dismisses-the-welcome-message-and-starts-the-match
key:Enter

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- round 1
until:1:60000:[L] NEW_ROUND
mark:ROUND-1-STARTED
# ~5 s of countdown follows NEW_ROUND before the cycles move.
wait:5500
# The HUD line "Enemies: N Friends: N" (gHud.cpp:479) is the live count of
# enemies still alive; at the top of a round it is the opponent count.
shot:04-round1-driving-HUD-shows-enemies
key:Left
wait:1200
shot:05-round1-after-LEFT
key:Right
wait:1200
shot:06-round1-after-RIGHT
until:1:150000:[L] ROUND_WINNER
mark:ROUND-1-COMPLETE
shot:07-round1-ended

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- round 2
until:2:60000:[L] NEW_ROUND
mark:ROUND-2-STARTED
wait:5500
shot:08-round2-driving-HUD-shows-enemies
key:Right
wait:1200
shot:09-round2-after-RIGHT
key:Left
wait:1200
shot:10-round2-after-LEFT
until:2:150000:[L] ROUND_WINNER
mark:ROUND-2-COMPLETE
shot:11-round2-ended

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- round 3
until:3:60000:[L] NEW_ROUND
mark:ROUND-3-STARTED
wait:5500
shot:12-round3-driving-HUD-shows-enemies
key:Left
wait:1200
shot:13-round3-after-LEFT
key:Left
wait:1200
shot:14-round3-after-LEFT-again
until:3:150000:[L] ROUND_WINNER
mark:ROUND-3-COMPLETE
shot:15-round3-ended

# SP_LIMIT_ROUNDS 3 means the match is over now (the shipped default is 10, so
# a match that stops here is itself the proof the setting was read). What comes
# next is welcome()'s second message and then the real main menu.
wait:8000
shot:16-after-the-match

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The frame-rate dump. Reports a DISTRIBUTION over the whole span of the
# rounds, per round and overall, and in two independent forms:
#
#   inst_fps       -- percentiles of 1000/(gap between consecutive frames).
#                     "min" is the single worst frame in the run, which is a
#                     harsh number: one 60 ms hitch anywhere sets it.
#   fps_per_second -- frames actually completed in each whole wall-clock second
#                     of the window, listed in full. This is "the frame rate"
#                     in the sense a player means it, and its MINIMUM is the
#                     worst full second of the run. The last, partial second of
#                     each window is dropped rather than reported as a dip.
#
# Both are reported because either alone can mislead: a median hides hitches, a
# single-frame minimum makes a smooth run look bad.
#
# THE BUCKET LOOP RUNS OVER SECONDS, NOT OVER OBSERVED KEYS, and that is
# load-bearing rather than style. An earlier version built the series by
# mapping over Object.keys() of the tally, so a second in which the game
# completed ZERO frames contributed no key and simply vanished from the series
# -- meaning the one number that exists to detect a stall was the one number
# that could not see a total freeze. Enumerating k = 0 .. floor(span) - 1 and
# defaulting to 0 makes a one-second hang read as fps_per_second_min = 0.
eval:(()=>{const S=window.__fps;if(!S)return 'no sampler';const all=Array.prototype.slice.call(S.t.subarray(0,S.n));const nr=S.marks.filter(m=>m[1].indexOf('[L] NEW_ROUND')===0).map(m=>m[0]);const rw=S.marks.filter(m=>m[1].indexOf('[L] ROUND_WINNER')===0).map(m=>m[0]);const q=(a,p)=>{if(!a.length)return null;const s=a.slice().sort((x,y)=>x-y);return s[Math.min(s.length-1,Math.max(0,Math.round(p*(s.length-1))))]};const r2=(x)=>(x===null||x===undefined)?null:Math.round(x*100)/100;const f=(v)=>(v&&v>0)?Math.round(100000/v)/100:null;const stats=(t0,t1)=>{const w=all.filter(x=>x>=t0&&x<=t1);const d=[];for(let i=1;i<w.length;i++)d.push(w[i]-w[i-1]);const sec={};for(const x of w){const k=Math.floor((x-t0)/1000);sec[k]=(sec[k]||0)+1}const span=(t1-t0)/1000;const nb=Math.floor(span);const per=[];for(let k=0;k<nb;k++)per.push(sec[k]||0);return{span_s:r2(span),frames:w.length,mean_fps:span>0?r2(w.length/span):null,frame_ms:{p50:r2(q(d,0.5)),p90:r2(q(d,0.9)),p99:r2(q(d,0.99)),max:r2(q(d,1))},inst_fps:{median:f(q(d,0.5)),p10:f(q(d,0.9)),p01:f(q(d,0.99)),min:f(q(d,1))},fps_per_second:per,fps_per_second_min:per.length?Math.min.apply(null,per):null,fps_per_second_median:q(per,0.5)}};const out={total_frames_sampled:S.n,rounds_started:nr.length,rounds_won:rw.length};const n=Math.min(nr.length,rw.length);if(n>0){out.overall=stats(nr[0],rw[n-1]);out.per_round=[];for(let i=0;i<n;i++)out.per_round.push(stats(nr[i],rw[i]))}return JSON.stringify(out)})()

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# POSITIVE CONTROL for the WebGL-warning subscription, and the reason it is
# here rather than in a comment claiming the subscription works.
#
# M1's Firefox transcript was read as clean when it was merely deaf
# (docs/porting/browser-runtime-notes.md section 9). A transcript with no WebGL
# warnings in it is worth nothing unless the same transcript can be shown to
# contain one when one is produced. So: make one, on purpose, at the very end
# of the run where it can affect nothing.
#
# The call is glHint(GL_PERSPECTIVE_CORRECTION_HINT, GL_FASTEST) -- byte for
# byte the call rScreen.cpp:1099 used to make on every menu frame before task 1
# guarded it, and the exact defect class section 9 is about. It runs on a
# THROWAWAY canvas with its own context, so it cannot spend the game's WebGL
# error budget or disturb anything on screen.
#
# WHAT THIS CONTROL DOES **NOT** TEST, so nobody reads more into it than is
# there: it does not exercise the glGetError poll. The sampler is wrapped
# around the GAME's context, which this canvas is not, and this step then calls
# getError() itself to print the code -- consuming the error a poll would have
# read. It tests the browser's warning channel and nothing else. Review
# established the other channel separately by injecting the same call on the
# game's own live context mid-run, which did produce
# "[GLERR] frame 540 glGetError=0x500". Folding that into this script is worth
# doing and is not free: it needs a safe point in the frame to fire at.
#
# PASS for this step: the transcript contains a browser-level warning line
# mentioning hint/INVALID_ENUM within a second of it. MEASURED RESULT: Chrome
# does ("[browser.warning/rendering] WebGL: INVALID_ENUM: hint: invalid
# target"); Firefox 154 does NOT, even with webgl.max-warnings-per-context
# raised and every log.entryAdded level recorded. That is why the sampler polls
# glGetError -- see the note at the top of this file. Keep this control: the
# day Firefox starts reporting them, this is what will notice.
mark:positive-control-deliberate-webgl-warning-follows
eval:(()=>{const c=document.createElement('canvas');const g=c.getContext('webgl');if(!g)return 'no webgl context for the control';g.hint(0x0C50,g.FASTEST);return 'deliberate bad glHint issued, gl.getError()=0x'+g.getError().toString(16)+' (0x500 == INVALID_ENUM)'})()
wait:1500
shot:17-final-state

# The GL-error tally from the game's own context over the whole run. A run with
# zero errors here is a real statement in both engines, which is exactly what
# M1's Firefox transcript could not make.
eval:(()=>{const S=window.__fps;if(!S)return 'no sampler';const codes={};for(const g of S.gl)codes['0x'+g[1].toString(16)]=(codes['0x'+g[1].toString(16)]||0)+1;return JSON.stringify({glGetError_polls:S.polls,glGetError_nonzero:S.gl.length,codes:codes,first:S.gl.slice(0,5)})})()

# SECOND POSITIVE CONTROL, and the reason it is last: it makes the page report
# an uncaught error, which web/shell.html:99 turns into a red failure banner.
# It answers "can this transcript see a browser-reported JS error at all?",
# which is what separates "Firefox reports nothing about WebGL specifically"
# from "Firefox reports nothing at all" -- and only the first of those is true.
mark:positive-control-deliberate-uncaught-error-follows
eval:setTimeout(function(){null.thisIsADeliberateUncaughtError;},0)
wait:2000
shot:18-after-deliberate-uncaught-error
